[{:seon.task/name "an-interrupt-leaves-nothing-pending"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.intr`'s pending atom is set when root's interrupt finds no eval to stop and cleared only by `seon.intr/clear-pending!`, which the nREPL interrupt op (`seon.repl`) calls and root's `seon.root/interrupt!` (SIGUSR1) never sends; a set pending makes `seon.intr/run` start stopped. Reproduction (jolt/wiki 8c50215, through the server; re-verify first): after `seon.root/interrupt!` stopped `(loop [] (recur))` with \"interrupted by root\", every later eval in that agent bounded by :timeout-ms 200 (and in its clone) answered \"interrupted by root\" in 0.3 ms instead of running to its bound. Not traced: why pending was set although an eval was running when SIGUSR1 came. Correct: an interrupt ends exactly the form it reached; with no form running it is recorded as a no-op, and nothing carries into the next form or into a clone. Deletes the pending carry-over."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.intr-test/an-interrupt-ends-only-the-form-it-reached seon.intr-test/a-clone-starts-with-nothing-pending}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-signal-wakes-a-blocked-wait"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged; Chez pinned at reference-code/chez `jolt/signal-wakes-wait`): Chez's `condition-wait` (s/prims.ss) runs pending signal handlers on its way out when interrupts are enabled, as the event handler does, so an agent whose main thread waits in condition-wait (a channel take, `(<!! (chan))`, a timeout) runs its SIGIO handler (`seon.intr/exit-at-eof!`) and ends with its root, and root's SIGUSR1 (`seon.root/interrupt!`) reaches it. Root's interrupt needed no Jolt change: Jolt's blocking `<!!` re-checks the thread's interrupt flag after every wake (java/async.ss ac-intr-wait!), so `seon.intr`'s SIGUSR1 handler also interrupts the current thread while an eval runs, and `seon.intr/run` answers \"interrupted by root\" whenever its token was set and clears the thread's flag when the eval ends (never the next request's). The cause was in Chez: a signal is delivered to the main thread only (c/schsig.c forward_signal_to_scheme) and its handler runs at the next event check, but pthread_cond_wait's spurious wakeup returned to a caller loop that re-waited before any check. Tried setting TRAP(tc) to 1 in S_condition_wait when a signal is pending; failed because the handler then ran only at the entry of the caller's next wait, after it had checked its predicate. Chez's mat signal-wakes-condition-wait: #f on fdd1e1e, #t after. No clock, thread or poll: the wakeup is the signal's own."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-blocked-agent-ends-on-its-socket-eof seon.agent-test/root-interrupts-a-blocked-take}}]
